{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790275659,"nonce":"rde9E+uZ2uvJIjQRNWE3f6PcJ3c=","title":"Interpolated strings: nested quotes inside ${} and escapes in the literal parts","message":"haxe/macro/Printer.hx failed the RD parser (\"expected expression, found ':'\") and fell back to the legacy parser: `' catch(${c.name}${c.type == null ? '' : (':' + ...)}) ...'`. The lexer ended a single-quoted string at the first `'`, even inside `${...}`; the part extractor matched braces without skipping strings. Both now skip interpolated code as code (nested braces, strings, and single-quoted strings with interpolations of their own).\n\nThe RD parser also kept `\\n` / `\\'` literally in an interpolated string's literal parts (the legacy parser unescaped them; Issue2244 only passed through the fallback).\n\nAlso: arrow functions accept `?a`, `a = default`, `?a:T = default` parameters (Issue9491, TestArrowFunctions); such an arrow is parsed as the equivalent function literal.","files":null}]}